.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1200 AM PDT Mon Aug 24 2026
(Today and tonight)

For the last couple of days we`ve written the tale of a battle
between the eastern Pacific trough and the Desert Southwest ridge
that has dominated our weather pattern. Today it`s the troughing
that will take the upper hand as a cutoff low inches towards the
Pacific Northwest, leading to slightly cooler temperatures and
cloudier mornings across the region. Another interesting factor
today is a weak shortwave trough coming into the region through the
morning. The two main global weather models, the European ECMWF and
the American GFS, are showing that there could be enough
instability, moisture, and lift to maybe, just maybe, support a few
high-based thunderstorms developing across the North Bay,
particularly over Napa County. This is a very low probability
forecast (around 5% chance through around 11 AM), along with a low
confidence for storm development. The higher resolution NAM, HRRR,
and RRFS models do not show any signs of developing storms, lapse
rates aloft are marginal at best, and K-Index values below 20
degrees Celsius suggest no chance for developing thunderstorms. Any
precipitation that does develop is also likely to end up as virga,
evaporating within the vast dry low layer where relative humidity
values as of the 0Z (5 PM PDT) OAK sounding fell into the single
digits.

Today`s highs range from the 80s into the lower 90s for the inland
valleys, the middle 90s to the triple digits across the warmest
interior spots, the upper 60s to the upper 70s near the Bays, and
the upper 50s to the middle 60s along the Pacific coast. The higher
elevations above the marine layer influence will remain rather day,
especially during the afternoon and evening hours. Breezy onshore
pattern winds will develop this afternoon and evening with wind
gusts reaching 20 to 30 mph along coastal jet regions, through
favored gaps and passes, and within the northern Salinas Valley.
Smoke from the Timber Fire will continue impacting the immediate
area around the actively burning fire through the day, with winds
aloft lofting the smoke plume over Salinas and Hollister. For air
quality information, refer to products issued by the Monterey Bay
Air Resources District.

