.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1200 AM PDT Mon Aug 24 2026
(Tuesday through Sunday)

Temperatures rebound Tuesday and Wednesday as the ridge reasserts
itself, with the inland valleys reaching highs in the middle 80s to
the middle 90s on Wednesday and Thursday while the Bays reach the
lower 70s to the lower 80s. A pattern change will begin later on
Thursday as a deep trough builds into the West Coast while the ridge
begins to inch its way eastwards towards the Southern Plains. It
will be interesting to see what the trough will do as the upcoming
weekend comes closer and we are better able to refine the forecast.
Also worth a mention is the possibility of tropical moisture coming
into the region, with our colleagues at the National Hurricane
Center tracking Tropical Storm Iselle and three other areas of
interest off the coast of Mexico. While the circulations remain far
to the south of our waters, the resultant moisture will be captured
by the developing trough with a wide variation in just how far north
the moisture goes. By way of example, the deterministic European
ECMWF model brings a swath of tropical moisture into the San
Francisco Bay Area by Friday morning, whereas the deterministic GFS
keeps the moisture plume to the south of Monterey County. In
addition, wave models are also showing an increase in southerly
swells as the tropical eastern Pacific remains active into the early
part of next week.

